MATHEMATICAL AND ARCHITECTURAL FOUNDATIONS FOR LOWER‑LIMB ROBOT MOVEMENT ANALYSIS: A UNIFIED FRAMEWORK INTEGRATING GEOMETRIC MECHANICS WITH LOCOMOTION DESIGN PRINCIPLES

Author: Ouadi Maakoul

---

Abstract

The human lower limb is an energetically sophisticated biomechanical system whose hierarchical chain of joints achieves stable, adaptive locomotion across varied terrain while recycling mechanical energy with efficiencies unmatched by robotic counterparts. Yet present approaches to understanding and replicating leg movement remain fragmented between mathematical control theory and physical design, lacking a shared vocabulary for mutual translation. This dissertation develops a unified theoretical framework that integrates advanced mathematical modelling with architectural and locomotion‑design principles to analyse, represent, and generate lower‑limb robot movements. By synthesising geometric mechanics—screw theory, Lie groups, hybrid port‑Hamiltonian systems, and contact mechanics—with spatial design thinking drawn from architecture and movement science, the work establishes an interdisciplinary language for describing leg kinematics, dynamics, and gait.

The framework introduces a formal locomotion grammar grounded in SE(3) whose primitives are screw motions, a hierarchical pattern language for gait archetypes, and tectonic principles with quantitative legibility proxies that connect structural form to human perception. A novel geometric characterisation of the stability–adaptability paradox is provided through a Stability Index SI(P) and a Terrain Coupling Index TCI(P,g), together with a two‑sided bounding theorem that links their product to the condition number of the actuation coupling matrix. Formal mappings between mathematical and design representations enable dual‑domain reasoning and theoretical design‑space exploration.

The dissertation is a purely theoretical contribution; no physical prototypes have been built and no empirical user studies have been conducted. The work provides a structured vocabulary and rigorous mathematical foundations for the design and analysis of prosthetic legs, rehabilitation exoskeletons, and energy‑efficient bipedal robots, while specifying complete protocols for the future empirical validation of its tectonic proxies.

Keywords: Leg kinematics, bipedal locomotion, geometric mechanics, screw theory, Lie groups, hybrid port‑Hamiltonian systems, contact mechanics, passive dynamic walking, series elastic actuation, locomotion grammar, pattern language, tectonics, stability–adaptability paradox, energetic efficiency.

