Chapter 1: Introduction

1.1 The Leg as a Bridge Between Mathematics and Design

The human lower limb is among evolution’s most refined engineering achievements. The hip–knee–ankle–foot chain provides a multi‑purpose platform for standing, walking, running, climbing, and recovering from perturbation—all within a single morphological structure driven by fewer independent muscles than mechanical degrees of freedom. Unlike the hand, whose primary function is manipulation and sensory exploration, the leg must simultaneously solve three deeply coupled problems: structural load bearing, forward propulsion, and terrain adaptation. Each constrains the others, creating a design space of extraordinary richness (Cavagna et al. 1977; Winter 1991; Perry & Burnfield 2010).

Mathematics provides the precision needed to describe leg movements in a principled way. The hip as a ball‑and‑socket joint modelled in SO(3), the hinge kinematics of the knee on SE(3) submanifolds, the complex subtalar mechanics of the ankle—all demand the full apparatus of geometric mechanics. Modern tools—screw theory, Lie groups, port‑Hamiltonian systems with discontinuous state resets at contact events—reveal deeper structures and enable principled design (Murray, Li & Sastry 1994; Duindam & Stramigioli 2006, 2009; Ames et al. 2014).

Design, meanwhile, brings human‑centred considerations to the fore. How does a robotic leg communicate its intent of motion to a human observer? How does a prosthetic leg achieve aesthetic coherence with the body while remaining legible in its mechanical function? How can structural form reveal mechanical logic? These questions sit at the intersection of architecture and design—disciplines that have long confronted the challenge of creating artefacts that are simultaneously functional, expressive, and adaptable (Alexander, Ishikawa & Silverstein 1977; Frampton 2001; Brand 1994). In prosthetics particularly, high abandonment rates correlate with perceived unnaturalness and opacity of function, underscoring the importance of legibility (Biddiss & Chau 2008).

Yet mathematics and design proceed along separate tracks in legged robotics. Mathematical models yield mechanically opaque designs whose locomotion strategy is hidden in control code rather than expressed in physical structure. Design solutions often lack the rigorous foundation needed for energetic optimisation and stability guarantees. This dissertation argues that a true understanding of leg movements—and the creation of artificial limbs that approach human performance—requires a synthesis of these two perspectives.

1.2 Problem Statement and Research Questions

The central problem is:

How can we develop a unified theoretical framework that integrates advanced mathematical modelling with architectural and design principles to analyse, represent, and generate lower‑limb robot movements?

This decomposes into four specific research questions:

RQ1 (Mathematical Foundation): How can geometric mechanics—specifically screw theory, Lie groups, hybrid port‑Hamiltonian systems, and contact mechanics—provide a complete description of leg kinematics and dynamics, capturing both continuous swing‑phase motion and the discrete impact events of heel‑strike and toe‑off, while building upon existing bipedal locomotion models (Spong 1996; Ames et al. 2014)?

RQ2 (Locomotion‑Design Language): What design‑oriented representations—locomotion grammars, gait pattern languages, tectonic principles—can capture the essential features of leg movements in a way that is intuitive for designers and compatible with mathematical formalisms?

RQ3 (Integration): How can mathematical and design representations be formally mapped to each other, enabling translation between analytical and synthetic modes of thinking?

RQ4 (Theoretical Implications): How does the integrated framework characterise and optimise the trade‑off between energetic efficiency and terrain adaptability, and what insights emerge for the design of prosthetic and robotic legs, particularly regarding underactuated and series‑elastic mechanisms?

1.3 Thesis Contributions and Originality

This dissertation makes seven original contributions—each validated against the closest known prior work and differentiated by a specific, identifiable advance.

Contribution 1 — A Unified Geometric–Design Framework for the Lower Limb. We present the first framework that couples a hybrid port‑Hamiltonian description of the full gait cycle—continuous swing and stance flows connected by momentum reset maps at heel‑strike and toe‑off—with design‑oriented representations (locomotion grammar, pattern language, tectonic proxies). Prior work (Ames et al. 2014; Duindam & Stramigioli 2006) established rigorous geometric modelling for bipeds but remained purely control‑ or simulation‑focused, containing no design vocabulary. The present framework adds design representations that allow prosthetists and rehabilitation engineers to reason about mechanism morphologies without engaging directly with Lie‑group differential equations.

Contribution 2 — Formal Characterisation of the Stability–Adaptability Paradox. We introduce a Stability Index SI(P) = \operatorname{rank}(P)/\dim(\ker(P^\top)) and a Terrain Coupling Index TCI(P,g) = \|P P^+ g\|/\|g\| that jointly quantify how underactuation distributes stabilisation authority and passive adaptation capacity across a limb. A two‑sided bounding theorem (Theorem 3.1) proves

\frac{1}{\kappa(P)} \cdot \frac{r}{n-r} \cdot \frac{\|g_1\|}{\|g\|}
\;\le\; SI(P)\,TCI(P,g)
\;\le\; \kappa(P) \cdot \frac{r}{n-r} \cdot \frac{\|g_1\|}{\|g\|},

where \kappa(P) is the condition number of the coupling matrix. When \kappa is small, the product SI\cdot TCI is tightly constrained around a value determined by the rank structure and synergy alignment; when \kappa is large, the bounds widen, illustrating that ill‑conditioned coupling is what makes the paradox binding. This is the first whole‑limb, coupling‑matrix‑level formalisation of the tension between active stabilisation and passive terrain compliance, complementing existing single‑joint variable‑stiffness analyses (Vanderborght et al. 2013).

Contribution 3 — A Locomotion Grammar with Geometric Grounding. We define a formal grammar G = (P,R,C) in which primitives are elements of SE(3) (one‑parameter screw motions of hip, knee, ankle, subtalar joints), rules compose them temporally (Seq, Par, Bilateral, Repeat), and constraints enforce physical feasibility (joint limits, centre‑of‑pressure bounds, friction‑cone wrench feasibility). This bridges the gap between continuous attractor‑based approaches (Ijspeert 2008) and abstract symbolic motion grammars by grounding every primitive in differential geometry while maintaining a human‑readable symbolic structure.

Contribution 4 — A Hierarchical Gait Pattern Language. We organise locomotion knowledge into a three‑tier pattern catalogue—joint‑scale, limb‑scale, and task‑scale patterns—each following the Alexandrian template (Problem, Solution, Mathematical Realisation, Contact‑Wrench Condition). While kinematic synergies (Ivanenko et al. 2004) capture similar regularities mathematically, they lack the contextual documentation and design guidance that the pattern language provides. The catalogue is directly usable by clinicians and engineers for mechanism specification.

Contribution 5 — Tectonic Proxies for Locomotion Legibility. We define four quantitative proxies—Actuator Visibility Index (AVI), Joint Articulation Exposure Score (JAES), Energy Flow Clarity (EFC), and Gait Intent Legibility Score (GILS)—that allow structural transparency to be optimised alongside mechanical performance. These extend the concept of motion legibility (Dragan, Lee & Srinivasa 2013) from abstract trajectory shaping to the morphological level, linking form to communicative intent. A complete three‑stage empirical validation protocol is specified for future work.

Contribution 6 — Formal Mappings and Dual Representation. A rigorous translation methodology between the mathematical description (joint trajectories, coupling matrix, Hamiltonian, reset maps) and the design description (gait pattern instances, grammar rules, tectonic scores) is developed, including a synthesis algorithm that converts a set of design requirements into a target coupling matrix via SVD assignment.

Contribution 7 — Theoretical Design‑Space Exploration. A parameterised family of leg models is used to derive analytical predictions from the energy balance: the U‑shape of specific resistance versus coupling ratio, the emergence of an optimal active‑passive energy split (~30–35 %) that matches biological tendon contributions, and the orthogonality of legibility metrics to mechanical performance. All numerical claims are derived from first‑principles expressions; simulation‑dependent quantities are explicitly flagged as requiring future computational validation.

Additionally, the framework introduces a corrected Gait Polyvalence Index GPI(D) = |T(D)|/(1 + m_D) that avoids division‑by‑zero singularities for purely passive designs, and a computational appendix (Appendix F) provides working pseudocode for forward kinematics, index computation, trajectory generation, and pattern classification, ensuring that all constructs are algorithmically concrete.

1.4 Scope, Validation Strategy, and Limitations

This is a purely theoretical dissertation. No physical prototypes have been built; no neural networks have been trained on empirical gait data; no user studies have been conducted. The contribution resides in the mathematical and design constructs themselves: indices, theorems, grammars, pattern catalogues, legibility metrics, and formal mappings. Every claim follows from the definitions and the geometric structure of the framework. Empirical validation—including a MuJoCo simulation to verify the analytical predictions and the three‑stage tectonic proxy study—is proposed as future work, with complete protocols provided (§4.3.4, §3B). The dissertation’s value is as a conceptual and mathematical foundation upon which subsequent experimental and computational studies can be built.

1.5 Outline

Chapter 2 surveys the literature across geometric mechanics, bipedal locomotion, series‑elastic actuation, and architectural design, identifying the specific gaps that the proposed framework fills. Chapter 3 develops the geometric framework for leg modelling—screw‑axis kinematics, hybrid port‑Hamiltonian dynamics, and the Stability–Adaptability Paradox with its two‑sided bounding theorem. Chapter 4 introduces the locomotion‑design framework: grammar, pattern language, and tectonic legibility proxies. Chapter 5 presents the formal mappings between the two representations, the synthesis algorithm, and the theoretical design‑space exploration. Chapter 6 concludes with a summary of contributions, limitations, and an agenda for future research. Appendices provide complete mathematical proofs, the full pattern catalogue, technical specifications, and computational pseudocode.

Chapter 2: Literature Review

The lower limb sits at the confluence of several disciplines—geometric mechanics, bipedal locomotion control, biomechanics, underactuated mechanism design, and architectural design theory. This review surveys each field not for its own sake, but to identify the specific gaps that the proposed framework fills. It is organised around the five gaps enumerated in §1.3; a summary table closes the chapter.

2.1 Geometric Mechanics for Legged Systems

2.1.1 Screw Theory and Lie Groups

Murray, Li and Sastry (1994) established the product‑of‑exponentials (POE) formula as the geometric foundation for manipulator kinematics. Their treatment of SE(3) as the configuration space of rigid bodies and the identification of joint screws as elements of the Lie algebra \mathfrak{se}(3) provides the language in which the lower limb is modelled in Chapter 3. Stramigioli, Maschke and Bidard (2000) extended this geometric picture to dynamics, giving a Hamiltonian formulation of spatial mechanisms that uses Lie groups and screw theory together with Dirac structures—a direct precursor to the port‑Hamiltonian approach used here. Featherstone (2008) developed articulated‑body inertia algorithms in a screw‑theoretic framework that remains the computational backbone of legged robot simulation.

For the lower limb specifically, the hip is a spherical joint (SO(3)), the knee an approximate revolute joint (S^1), and the ankle a compound of talocrural and subtalar revolute axes. The configuration space is therefore a product of Lie groups, as developed in §3.1. Classical Denavit–Hartenberg formulations obscure these symmetries; the POE formulation makes them explicit.

2.1.2 Port‑Hamiltonian and Hybrid Systems

Van der Schaft and Maschke (2002) introduced port‑Hamiltonian systems as a geometric formalism that represents energy storage, dissipation, and exchange through explicit power ports—ideal for legged locomotion where energy flows between the robot, elastic elements, and the ground. Duindam and Stramigioli (2006, 2009) applied this formalism to bipedal walking robots, providing the closest prior work to the continuous‑flow portion of the framework developed in Chapter 3. Their framework optimises both trajectories and mechanical structure but contains no design vocabulary, no locomotion grammar, and no tectonic proxies.

The crucial extension for legged locomotion is the hybrid port‑Hamiltonian formulation, because the gait cycle alternates continuous phases (swing, stance) with discrete impact events (heel‑strike, toe‑off). Ames et al. (2014) developed Hybrid Zero Dynamics (HZD), a geometric control framework that stabilises periodic orbits in hybrid systems with impulse effects, and demonstrated it on bipedal robots. HZD is a control‑synthesis tool for a fixed morphology; it does not address mechanism design. Farid and Ruggiero (2023) have recently investigated disturbance reconstruction for hybrid port‑Hamiltonian dynamics of biped robots, confirming the continued vitality of this approach.

2.1.3 Underactuation and the Coupling Matrix

The hand dissertation on which the present work is built introduced a formal characterisation of underactuation through the coupling matrix P\in\mathbb{R}^{n\times m}, where n is the number of joints and m the number of actuators, and defined the Adaptation Index AI=(n-r)/r with r=\operatorname{rank}(P). The hand prioritises passive adaptation (high AI is desirable); the leg inverts this priority—active stabilisation is paramount. The Stability Index SI(P)=r/(n-r) introduced in §3.4 is the reciprocal of AI, reflecting this inverted design logic. No prior work has formalised this structural‑level stability metric for legged mechanisms. The underactuated hands literature (Dollar & Howe 2010; Birglen et al. 2008) uses the same coupling‑matrix mathematics but focuses exclusively on grasping, never on locomotion.

2.2 Bipedal Locomotion and the Stability–Adaptability Tension

2.2.1 Passive Dynamic Walking and Series‑Elastic Actuation

McGeer (1990) demonstrated that a purely passive, unactuated biped could walk stably down a gentle slope—proving that stable gait can emerge from mechanical design alone. Collins et al. (2005) built on this insight to create efficient quasi‑passive bipeds requiring only small actuating impulses. These results establish that morphology and passive dynamics are at least as important as control, motivating a framework that treats mechanism design and control on equal footing.

Pratt and Williamson (1995) introduced series elastic actuators (SEAs), placing a compliant spring between motor and load. For legged systems, SEAs provide shock absorption at impact, accurate force control, and passive energy storage—mirroring biological tendon function.

2.2.2 Variable Stiffness Actuators and the Compliance–Stability Trade‑off

The tension between stability (requiring high stiffness) and adaptability (requiring compliance) has been extensively studied in the variable stiffness actuator (VSA) literature. Vanderborght et al. (2013) provided a comprehensive review of actuators with passively adjustable compliance. The AMASC actuator (Hurst et al. 2010) showed that variable leg stiffness is critical for effective gait control. All of this work, however, addresses the trade‑off at the single‑joint level through stiffness‑bandwidth metrics. What is absent is a whole‑limb, coupling‑matrix‑level characterisation—how the rank and singular‑value structure of P simultaneously determines the limb’s collective stability authority and its terrain‑adaptation capacity. The Stability Index SI(P) and Terrain Coupling Index TCI(P,g) introduced in §3.4 fill this gap.

2.2.3 Specific Resistance and Energetic Benchmarks

Specific resistance SR = E/(mgd) (energy per unit weight per unit distance) is the standard dimensionless metric for locomotion economy. Human walking achieves SR\approx0.05–0.08; the passive dynamic walker of Collins et al. (2005) achieved 0.055; MIT’s Spring Flamingo (Pratt & Williamson 1995) approximately 0.20; Boston Dynamics’ ATLAS approximately 0.35. These benchmarks anchor the design‑space exploration in §3.5.

2.3 Locomotion‑Design Languages

2.3.1 Central Pattern Generators and Motion Primitives

Central Pattern Generators (CPGs) are mathematical models of the rhythmic neural circuits that produce locomotion in vertebrates. Ijspeert (2008) established CPGs as a design methodology for legged robot control, showing that coupled oscillators can generate stable walking patterns without explicit trajectory planning. CPGs generate locomotion as continuous attractor dynamics of coupled oscillators—they provide temporal structure but no geometric content. The locomotion grammar of Chapter 4 differs precisely by grounding every primitive in the screw geometry of the corresponding joint, giving designers a direct mapping from a symbolic rule to the physical motion it produces.

2.3.2 Pattern Languages in Architecture and Design

Christopher Alexander, Ishikawa and Silverstein (1977) introduced the pattern language as a structured catalogue of design solutions, each consisting of a problem statement, a discussion, and a solution. Pattern languages have been applied to software engineering (Gamma et al. 1995) and human–computer interaction, but never to locomotion design. Ivanenko et al. (2004, 2006) discovered that muscle activity during human locomotion can be accounted for by a small number of basic temporal activation patterns (kinematic synergies). These synergies are the biomechanical analogue of design patterns, but they are presented as principal‑component decompositions, not as a design‑accessible catalogue with problem–solution structure and contact‑wrench conditions. The pattern language of §4.2 provides exactly this translation.

2.3.3 Tectonics and Expressive Structure

Frampton (2001) defined tectonics as “the expressive potential of constructional technique”—the capacity of a building’s structure to communicate its own making and logic. Applied to robotic legs, the tectonic principle holds that springs, actuators, and force paths should be expressed rather than concealed. This has direct relevance to prosthetics: cosmetic covers have been shown to significantly reduce body‑image disturbances and improve psychosocial adjustment (Jönköping University study, 2025). Yet cosmetic covers often conceal mechanical function. The tectonic approach offers a middle path: structural honesty that is simultaneously aesthetically coherent.

2.3.4 Legibility in Human–Robot Interaction

Dragan, Lee and Srinivasa (2013) formalised the distinction between predictability and legibility of robot motion. Their formalism defines legibility as the probability of the goal given the observed trajectory. This work addresses motion legibility—how trajectory shape communicates intent. The present framework extends legibility to the morphological level: the Gait Intent Legibility Score (GILS) introduced in §4.3 quantifies how well a leg’s form communicates its function, complementing the trajectory‑level legibility of Dragan et al. No prior work has linked Frampton’s architectural tectonics to Dragan’s HRI legibility in the context of prosthetic or robotic leg design.

