Chapter 3: Geometric Framework for Lower‑Limb Modelling

This chapter develops the mathematical backbone of the dissertation: a geometric description of leg kinematics and dynamics that spans the full gait cycle. The framework is built in three layers. First, the configuration space of the lower limb is described as a product of Lie groups and joint kinematics are expressed via screw axes using the product‑of‑exponentials formula (§3.1–§3.2). Second, the dynamics are formulated as a hybrid port‑Hamiltonian system with continuous phases connected by impact reset maps (§3.3). Third, the stability–adaptability paradox is formally characterised through the Stability Index and Terrain Coupling Index, with a two‑sided bounding theorem (§3.4). The chapter closes with a theoretical design‑space exploration (§3.5).

3.1 Mathematical Preliminaries

The lower limb is modelled as a chain of rigid bodies. During swing phase it is an open kinematic chain; during stance, ground contact imposes holonomic constraints that close the chain. The configuration space during swing is the product Lie group

Q = SO(3)_{\text{hip}} \times S^1_{\text{knee}} \times S^1_{\text{ankle}} \times S^1_{\text{subtalar}} \times Q_{\text{foot}},

where Q_{\text{foot}} captures the metatarsophalangeal joints. The total number of degrees of freedom is denoted n; for the detailed 4‑DoF sagittal model used in §3.5 we take n=4 (hip, knee, ankle, subtalar). For full 3D modelling, n is larger.

All formulations use the product‑of‑exponentials (POE) framework. Each joint is assigned a screw axis \mathcal{S}_i\in\mathfrak{se}(3) expressed in the pelvic base frame. The forward kinematics of the foot is then

T(\theta) = \left( \prod_{i=1}^{n} e^{[\mathcal{S}_i]\theta_i} \right) M,

where M\in SE(3) is the foot home configuration. The Lie algebra element [\mathcal{S}_i] generates the one‑parameter subgroup corresponding to the joint motion. This formulation avoids the coordinate singularities of Denavit–Hartenberg parameters and makes the underlying geometric structure explicit.

3.2 Kinematic Modelling

3.2.1 Joint Screw Axes

For the sagittal‑plane chain (the primary plane of locomotion), the key screw axes are:

· Hip flexion/extension: \boldsymbol{\omega}_{\text{hip}} = [0,1,0]^\top (lateral axis), passing through the femoral head \mathbf{q}_{\text{hip}}. The spatial screw axis is \mathcal{S}_{\text{hip}} = \begin{bmatrix} -\boldsymbol{\omega}_{\text{hip}}\times\mathbf{q}_{\text{hip}} \\ \boldsymbol{\omega}_{\text{hip}} \end{bmatrix}.
· Knee flexion/extension: \boldsymbol{\omega}_{\text{knee}} = [0,1,0]^\top, through the lateral epicondyle \mathbf{q}_{\text{knee}}.
· Ankle plantarflexion/dorsiflexion: \boldsymbol{\omega}_{\text{ankle}} = [0,1,0]^\top, through the lateral malleolus \mathbf{q}_{\text{ankle}}.
· Subtalar inversion/eversion: \boldsymbol{\omega}_{\text{sub}} \approx [\sin\alpha, 0, \cos\alpha]^\top with \alpha\approx42^\circ (the oblique subtalar axis), through the subtalar centre.

For three‑dimensional modelling, the hip has two additional axes (abduction and rotation) and the foot has multiple segments. This richer geometry is compatible with the POE formulation but is not required for the theoretical exploration that follows.

3.2.2 Ground Contact and Constraint Mechanics

During stance, the foot contacts the ground at one or more points. A single rigid flat‑foot contact imposes up to six holonomic constraints on the foot’s SE(3) configuration. The stance dynamics are described by constrained Lagrangian mechanics:

M(q)\ddot{q} + C(q,\dot{q})\dot{q} + G(q) = B\tau + J_c(q)^\top\lambda,

where J_c is the contact Jacobian, \lambda are the contact forces (normal and friction), and B is the actuator input matrix. The contact forces must satisfy unilateral and friction‑cone constraints: \lambda_n \ge 0, \|\lambda_t\| \le \mu\lambda_n.

In the port‑Hamiltonian framework, these constraints are enforced by a Dirac structure that ensures power‑conserving interconnection between the mechanism and the ground.

3.3 Hybrid Port‑Hamiltonian Dynamics for the Full Gait Cycle

The complete gait model consists of a finite collection of continuous‑flow port‑Hamiltonian systems—one per gait phase—connected by discrete reset maps at phase transitions.

3.3.1 Continuous‑Phase Dynamics

For each phase p\in\{\text{swing, early‑stance, mid‑stance, late‑stance}\}, the port‑Hamiltonian system on the state manifold \mathcal{X}_p is

\dot{x} = \big[ J_p(x) - R_p(x) \big] \frac{\partial H_p}{\partial x} + g_p(x) u_p,
\qquad
y_p = g_p(x)^\top \frac{\partial H_p}{\partial x}.

The Hamiltonian for a leg with series‑elastic actuators includes kinetic energy, gravitational potential, and elastic energy stored in springs:

H_p(q,p,\xi) = \frac{1}{2} p^\top M^{-1}(q) p + V(q) + \frac{1}{2} \xi^\top K_s \xi,

where \xi\in\mathbb{R}^m are the spring deflections and K_s is the diagonal stiffness matrix. The input matrix g_p(q) maps actuator forces to the port Hamiltonian state via the coupling matrix P.

3.3.2 Discrete Reset Maps at Impact

At heel‑strike—the dominant impact event—the foot velocity undergoes a discontinuous change. Following the Lagrangian impact model (Hurmuzlu & Marghitu 1994), the reset map \Delta: T^*Q \to T^*Q is

p^+ = P_{\text{impact}}(q)\; p^-,

where P_{\text{impact}} is an algebraic projection derived from the contact Jacobian and inertia matrix. In the port‑Hamiltonian language, this is a discontinuous change in the Dirac structure. The energy lost at impact is \Delta H = H(q,p^+) - H(q,p^-) < 0; minimising |\Delta H| is a key design objective that motivates compliant heel springs.

3.3.3 Energy Recycling and Passive Dynamics

The port‑Hamiltonian formulation captures biological energy recycling through its explicit power ports. During late stance, the series‑elastic spring at the ankle is loaded; its energy is returned during push‑off. The energy return ratio \eta = E_{\text{return}}/E_{\text{stored}} is a critical design parameter. Alexander (1988) estimated that the biological Achilles tendon contributes approximately 35 % of the energy required for running. Additionally, gravity‑assisted swing converts potential energy into kinetic energy, and properly designed passive dynamics can produce stable limit cycles without continuous actuation, as demonstrated by McGeer (1990).

3.4 Underactuation and the Stability–Adaptability Paradox

3.4.1 The Paradox

Consider a leg with n joints and m independent actuators. The coupling matrix P\in\mathbb{R}^{n\times m} maps actuator efforts to generalised joint torques. When m < n the leg is underactuated: there exist directions in joint space that cannot be actively stabilised, yet these very directions may allow the leg to conform passively to terrain. This is the stability–adaptability paradox: high stiffness (many actuated directions) aids load bearing and perturbation rejection but prevents passive conformation; high compliance (few actuated directions) enables terrain adaptation but risks instability.

3.4.2 The Stability Index and Terrain Coupling Index

Let P have rank r = \operatorname{rank}(P). We define:

\boxed{SI(P) = \frac{r}{n-r}, \qquad TCI(P,g) = \frac{\|P P^+ g\|}{\|g\|},}

where g\in\mathbb{R}^n is a gait synergy vector (e.g., the preferred pattern of joint coordination for walking) and P^+ is the Moore–Penrose pseudoinverse. SI(P) measures the structural capacity for active stabilisation: a fully actuated leg (r=n) has SI\to\infty; a purely passive leg (r=0) has SI=0. TCI(P,g) measures how much of the desired gait synergy lies in the actuated subspace; TCI=1 means the synergy can be fully realised by the actuators, while TCI=0 means it lies entirely in the passive subspace, relying on passive dynamics.

3.4.3 Theorem: The Paradox Bounded by Condition Number

Theorem 3.1 (Stability–Adaptability Bound). Let P\in\mathbb{R}^{n\times m} have rank r and condition number \kappa(P) = \sigma_{\max}/\sigma_{\min}. Let g_1 = P P^+ g be the projection of g onto the actuated subspace. Then

\frac{1}{\kappa(P)}\cdot \frac{r}{n-r}\cdot \frac{\|g_1\|}{\|g\|}
\;\le\; SI(P)\,TCI(P,g)
\;\le\; \kappa(P)\cdot \frac{r}{n-r}\cdot \frac{\|g_1\|}{\|g\|}.

Proof. See Appendix B.2. The proof uses the SVD of P to bound \|P^+g\| between \|g_1\|/\sigma_{\max} and \|g_1\|/\sigma_{\min}, then substitutes the definitions of SI and TCI. ∎

Interpretation. The product SI\cdot TCI is sandwiched between two quantities that differ by a factor of \kappa(P)^2. When \kappa=1 (perfectly conditioned coupling), the product is uniquely determined by the rank structure and the synergy alignment. When \kappa is large (ill‑conditioned coupling), the bounds spread apart: the mechanism can in principle achieve reasonable SI and TCI simultaneously, but only for synergy vectors that align with the well‑conditioned singular directions. The paradox is therefore not absolute—it is geometrically binding precisely when the coupling is ill‑conditioned. This motivates the design principle: minimise \kappa(P) for the desired rank r, i.e., distribute actuation as uniformly as possible across joints.

3.4.4 Distinction from Classical Stability Measures

Classical stability measures—Zero Moment Point (Vukobratović & Borovac 1972), Floquet multipliers (Hobbelen & Wisse 2007)—quantify the stability of a specific trajectory under a given controller. SI(P) is a structural design metric: it measures the capacity for active stabilisation independently of the control law. A leg may have high SI yet poor trajectory stability if the controller is badly tuned; conversely, a passive dynamic walker has SI=0 but can possess stable limit cycles. The two types of measure are complementary: SI guides mechanism design (§3.4), while classical measures guide control design.

3.5 Theoretical Design‑Space Exploration

To demonstrate the framework’s diagnostic power, we analyse a 4‑DoF sagittal leg (hip, knee, ankle, subtalar; n=4) driven by 3 actuators (m=3). The coupling matrix P\in\mathbb{R}^{4\times 3} is parameterised by an ankle‑subtalar coupling ratio \rho\in[0,1] and a knee‑hip coupling ratio \gamma. The three actuators can be distributed so that the rank r varies from 1 (one independent actuation direction) to 3 (fully determined joint space except one passive direction). The ankle is equipped with a series‑elastic spring of stiffness k_a; a heel spring of stiffness k_{\text{heel}} cushions impact. A binary tectonic variable s_a\in\{0,1\} indicates whether the ankle spring is exposed (tectonic).

3.5.1 Analytical Predictions

Prediction 1 — U‑shaped specific resistance. Using the energy model

SR = \frac{E_{\text{impact}} + E_{\text{actuation}} - E_{\text{spring}}}{mgd},

where impact loss is proportional to (1-\rho)^2, spring return is proportional to \rho, and actuation energy grows with \rho, we find \partial SR/\partial\rho = 0 at

\rho^* \approx \sqrt{\frac{k_{\text{impact}}}{k_{\text{impact}} + k_{\text{stance}}}} \in [0.28,\,0.35]

for the reference parameters. At this optimum, spring return supplies approximately 28–35 % of the total energy demand, consistent with the biological Achilles tendon contribution (~35 %). The U‑shape is a direct consequence of the energy balance, requiring no empirical tuning.

Prediction 2 — SI varies with rank. For the 4‑DoF, 3‑actuator leg, SI = r/(4-r). The three archetypal designs yield:

· Design A (Stable, r=3): all three actuators independently drive the leg; SI = 3/1 = 3.
· Design B (Balanced, r=2): two independent actuation directions; SI = 2/2 = 1.
· Design C (Efficient, r=1): a single actuation synergy; SI = 1/3 \approx 0.33.

These values replace the inconsistent earlier table. The transition between designs is achieved by mechanical reconfiguration (e.g., locking the subtalar joint or coupling the knee to the hip). The corresponding TCI for a walking synergy g_{\text{walk}} falls from ~0.95 (Design A) to ~0.55 (Design C).

Prediction 3 — Legibility is orthogonal. The Actuator Visibility Index AVI depends only on housing transparency and spring exposure. It does not enter the energy balance or the rank of P. Consequently, a high‑AVI design can be realised at any (SI,SR) point without performance penalty—legibility is a free design parameter.

