3.5.3 Terrain Robustness Implications

From the definitions, a design with SI<0.5 (i.e., r=1 for n=4) has only one actuated direction; disturbances that push the leg outside that synergy cannot be actively rejected and must be absorbed passively. Such designs are expected to recover slowly on irregular terrain, requiring several steps to re‑establish steady gait. Conversely, designs with SI>2 (e.g., r=3 with n=4) have three independent stabilising directions and should recover in one or two steps on moderate terrain. These qualitative predictions will be tested in the MuJoCo simulation specified in the methodology (Chapter 3B); the present section provides their theoretical foundation.

Chapter 4: Locomotion‑Design Framework

Where Chapter 3 provides the mathematical description of the leg—kinematics, hybrid port‑Hamiltonian dynamics, and the stability–adaptability indices—this chapter provides the design language through which a prosthetist, rehabilitation engineer, or industrial designer can reason about leg movements without engaging directly with Lie‑group differential equations. The framework has three components: a locomotion grammar (§4.1), a hierarchical pattern language (§4.2), and tectonic principles with quantitative legibility proxies (§4.3).

4.1 Locomotion Grammar for Leg Movements

4.1.1 Algebraic Formalisation

Definition 4.1 (Locomotion Grammar). A locomotion grammar is a triple G = (P,R,C) where:

Primitives P are movement atoms defined at the joint or limb level. Each primitive corresponds to a one‑parameter subgroup of SE(3) generated by a joint screw axis \mathcal{S}_j:

· Flex(\phi, j): rotate joint j by angle \phi about its screw axis \mathcal{S}_j.
· Extend(\phi, j): reverse rotation by angle \phi.
· LoadAbsorb(j): controlled flexion of joint j under external load (impact absorption).
· PushOff(\tau, j): apply an impulsive torque \tau at joint j (toe‑off).
· SwingClear(j): coordinate hip and knee to lift the foot.
· Contact(f, p): foot segment f makes ground contact at point p; triggers the impact reset map and activates contact constraints.
· Release(f): foot segment f loses ground contact.

The geometric content is explicit: Flex(\phi,j) produces the motion \exp([\mathcal{S}_j]\phi). This distinguishes the grammar from CPG‑based approaches, which generate continuous attractor dynamics without geometric interpretation.

Rules R compose primitives temporally and spatially:

· Seq(\alpha, \beta): sequential execution—\beta follows \alpha.
· Par(\alpha, \beta): parallel (simultaneous) execution.
· Bilateral(\alpha): apply \alpha to both limbs with a phase offset of 50 % of the gait cycle.
· Repeat(\alpha, n): n‑fold repetition (cyclic gait).
· PhaseShift(\alpha, \varphi): apply \alpha with phase offset \varphi \in [0,1] relative to the cycle.

Constraints C enforce physical realizability:

· Joint limits: \theta_j^{\min} \le \theta_j \le \theta_j^{\max}.
· Centre of pressure (COP) containment within the support polygon.
· Contact wrench feasibility: \lambda_n > 0, \|\lambda_t\| \le \mu \lambda_n.
· Foot clearance during swing: h(\theta) \ge \varepsilon_{\text{clear}}.
· Energy budget: \int u^\top \dot{\theta} \, dt \le E_{\max} per cycle.

4.1.2 Worked Example: Generating a Walking Gait

Starting from the double‑support configuration, a single step is generated by the derivation:

1. WeightShift: Par(Extend(5^\circ, hip_L), LoadAbsorb(knee_R))
2. PushOff(\tau_{\text{push}}, ankle_R)
3. SwingClear(L): Par(Flex(30^\circ, hip_L), Flex(60^\circ, knee_L))
4. LandingPrep(L): Par(Extend(30^\circ, knee_L), Flex(10^\circ, ankle_L))
5. Contact(heel_L, p_L) — discrete event triggering the impact reset map.

The full walking cycle is then Bilateral(Seq(WeightShift, PushOff, SwingClear, LandingPrep, Contact)).

Running replaces Contact(heel) with Contact(forefoot) and adds a Leap primitive to generate a flight phase. Stair ascent adds a VerticalLift(\Delta h) primitive that incorporates the step‑height constraint into the grammar. The grammar therefore generates all standardised gaits—walking, running, stair ascent, stair descent, and perturbed recovery—by suitable choice of primitive angles and rule sequences (Winter 1991; Perry & Burnfield 2010). The constraint set C ensures that every generated motion respects joint ranges, friction limits, and energy budgets.

4.1.3 Expressiveness Argument

We argue that G = (P,R,C) is sufficiently expressive to generate all gaits in the standard Winter (1991) and Perry & Burnfield (2010) taxonomies. Every standardised gait is defined by a piecewise‑smooth sequence of joint‑angle profiles over the gait phases. The primitives \{Flex, Extend, LoadAbsorb, PushOff, SwingClear, Contact, Release\} span all sign‑definite joint motions; because joint angles are continuous and each primitive can be instantiated with any angle within the anatomical limits, the reachable set of joint‑space trajectories under the Seq and Par rules is dense in the space of piecewise‑smooth trajectories on the configuration manifold Q. The Bilateral rule imposes anti‑phase bilateral symmetry; Repeat enforces periodicity. The constraint set C filters this dense set to only those trajectories that satisfy physical feasibility (COP containment, friction cone, clearance). Consequently, for any gait in the standard taxonomy—which by definition is a feasible periodic trajectory—there exists a grammar derivation that approximates it to arbitrary precision.

The grammar does not guarantee a unique canonical derivation for each gait; different derivations may encode different clinical or design interpretations of the same movement. This ambiguity is a feature, not a limitation: it reflects genuine multiplicity in how human locomotion can be described. A formal denseness proof in the sense of metric topology—specifying a norm on the trajectory space and showing that the \varepsilon‑ball around any feasible trajectory contains a grammar‑generated one—is deferred to future work, in the mode of the analogous argument advanced in the hand dissertation.

4.2 Pattern Language for Gait and Locomotion

The pattern language organises locomotion knowledge into a three‑tier hierarchy: joint‑scale patterns (physical actions of individual joints), limb‑scale patterns (coordinated behaviour of the whole leg), and task‑scale patterns (complete locomotion tasks). Each pattern follows the Alexandrian template: Problem, Solution, Mathematical Realisation, Contact‑Wrench Condition, Restraining Forces, Related Patterns. A subset is presented here; the complete catalogue of 22 patterns appears in Appendix C.

4.2.1 Joint‑Scale Patterns

J1: Heel‑Strike Absorption

· Problem: Uncontrolled heel impact generates peak ground reaction forces exceeding three times body weight, risking joint damage and energy waste.
· Solution: Controlled knee flexion (loading response) from 0^\circ to \sim15^\circ, combined with a compliant heel spring, dissipates impact energy gradually.
· Mathematical Realisation: Impact reset map p^+ = P_{\text{impact}}(q)\, p^-; energy loss \Delta H = H(q,p^+) - H(q,p^-). The heel spring stiffness k_{\text{heel}} is chosen to minimise |\Delta H|. The knee flexion reduces the effective mass seen by the ground.
· Contact‑Wrench Condition: \lambda_n > 0, \|\lambda_t\| \le \mu \lambda_n.

J2: Push‑Off Impulse

· Problem: Insufficient propulsive force at toe‑off reduces walking speed; excessive force wastes energy.
· Solution: Rapid ankle plantarflexion powered by stored elastic energy in the series‑elastic element plus a short active burst.
· Mathematical Realisation: Ankle velocity reaches \dot\theta_{\text{ankle}} \approx 300^\circ/\text{s}; elastic energy E_{\text{spring}} = \frac12 k_a \xi^2 is returned during push‑off. The work performed is approximately 0.2\,\text{J/kg}. The energy return ratio \eta = E_{\text{return}}/E_{\text{stored}} \ge 0.85.
· Related Patterns: L2 (Energy Return Cycle).

J3: Swing Clearance

· Problem: The foot must clear the ground and any obstacles without excessive hip circumduction, which signals prosthetic compensation and reduces gait legibility.
· Solution: Coordinated hip flexion (25^\circ–35^\circ) and knee flexion (55^\circ–65^\circ) at mid‑swing, achieving at least 1 cm foot clearance on level ground.
· Mathematical Realisation: Clearance h(\theta) = f(\theta_{\text{hip}},\theta_{\text{knee}},\dots) \ge \varepsilon_{\text{clear}}. The peak knee flexion occurs at 73 % of swing phase (Winter 1991 normative data).

J4: Subtalar Terrain Adaptation

· Problem: A rigid foot cannot maintain full plantar contact on laterally inclined or uneven surfaces.
· Solution: Passive subtalar inversion/eversion with low stiffness allows the foot to conform without active control.
· Mathematical Realisation: The subtalar joint has a low‑rank coupling—its row in P lies near the passive subspace, giving low local SI. Stiffness k_{\text{sub}} \in [5,50]\,\text{Nm/rad} ensures adaptation while preventing excessive roll.
· Contact‑Wrench Condition: Full foot contact is maintained if k_{\text{sub}} is correctly tuned.

4.2.2 Limb‑Scale Patterns

L1: Stance Stiffening

· Problem: The stance leg must support full body weight without large muscular effort.
· Solution: Exploit passive alignment: the knee remains near full extension during mid‑stance, transmitting load through the skeletal chain with minimal active torque (inverted pendulum mode).
· Mathematical Realisation: \theta_{\text{knee}} \in [0^\circ,15^\circ]; leg stiffness k_{\text{leg}} \approx 10–25\,\text{kN/m}. SI is high in this configuration, while ZMP remains within the support polygon.
· Related Patterns: J1, L2.

L2: Energy Return Cycle

· Problem: The gait cycle should minimise net actuator work by recovering energy that would otherwise be dissipated.
· Solution: A series‑elastic element stores energy during stance braking and returns it during push‑off, analogous to the Achilles tendon.
· Mathematical Realisation: Port‑Hamiltonian energy balance: \dot H = -\partial H^\top R\,\partial H + y^\top u - \dot W_{\text{spring}}. The total cycle efficiency \eta_{\text{cycle}} = E_{\text{push-off}}/(E_{\text{impact}}+E_{\text{active}}) reaches \sim 0.85 in well‑designed elastic elements, close to the biological contribution of \sim 35\% (Alexander 1988).

L3: Swing Phase Optimisation

· Problem: Actively driving all joints through the swing phase wastes energy.
· Solution: Use the passive pendulum dynamics of the swing leg: hip flexion initiates the swing, and knee and ankle follow naturally through inertial and gravitational coupling.
· Mathematical Realisation: The natural frequency \omega_n = \sqrt{g/l_{\text{leg}}} is matched to the desired cadence; active torques are limited to <20\% of maximum.

L5: Terrain Conformation

· Problem: Fixed‑stiffness legs cannot adapt to terrain variation without explicit sensing.
· Solution: Design a stiffness gradient: high active stiffness proximally (hip, knee) for stabilisation; low passive stiffness distally (ankle, subtalar) for terrain compliance. The SI is high proximally and low distally.
· Mathematical Realisation: The coupling matrix P is designed with an intentional SI gradient, and TCI is tuned to the walking synergy g_{\text{walk}}.

4.2.3 Task‑Scale Patterns

Each task‑scale pattern composes limb‑ and joint‑scale patterns into a full movement strategy.

T1: Level Walking is generated by Bilateral(Seq(J1, L1, J2, L3, J3, LandingPrep, Contact)). Specific resistance SR\approx 0.08–0.20.

T2: Stair Ascent uses HighKneeFlex(80^\circ), contact with the toe, a powerful knee drive, and increased push‑off impulse.

T3: Running replaces the inverted‑pendulum stance with a spring‑mass model (Blickhan 1989) and inserts a flight phase. SR\approx 0.05—energetically cheaper than walking at high speeds.

T5: Perturbation Recovery employs capture‑point theory (Pratt et al. 2006). The grammar rule becomes a conditional branch: if a perturbation is detected, execute a rapid foot‑placement correction to the capture point r_{\text{capture}} = \dot x_{\text{COM}}/\omega_n.

4.3 Tectonics and Locomotion Legibility

The tectonic principle (Frampton 2001) holds that the expressive potential of constructional logic should be celebrated rather than concealed. For a robotic or prosthetic leg, this means that springs, cables, and actuators should be visible, and the load path should be legible in the physical form. This is not merely an aesthetic choice: it directly addresses the problem of prosthetic abandonment, which correlates with perceived unnaturalness and functional opacity (Biddiss & Chau 2008; Jönköping University 2025).

4.3.1 Quantitative Legibility Proxies

We define four measurable indices that translate tectonic qualities into numbers that can be optimised alongside mechanical performance.

Actuator Visibility Index (AVI):

AVI = \frac{L_{\text{visible}}}{L_{\text{total}}} \cdot \frac{1}{1 + \text{opacity}},

where L_{\text{visible}} and L_{\text{total}} are the exposed and total lengths of the actuation elements (e.g., springs, cables), and opacity \in [0,1] quantifies housing transparency.

Joint Articulation Exposure Score (JAES):

JAES = \frac{\sum_i w_i \cdot (\text{exposed DoF}_i)}{\sum_i w_i \cdot (\text{total DoF}_i)},

with weights w_i reflecting biomechanical importance.

Energy Flow Clarity (EFC):

EFC = \frac{1}{1 + \text{path\_curvature} + \#\text{hidden\_springs}},

where path curvature penalises convoluted cable/spring routing.

Gait Intent Legibility Score (GILS):

GILS = \frac{1}{1 + \sum_k w_k \cdot d_k(\text{observed motion}, \text{motion predicted from morphology})}.

This extends Dragan et al.’s (2013) trajectory‑level legibility to the morphological level: an observer should be able to predict the robot’s next action from its static form. High GILS implies that the leg’s structure clearly communicates its locomotion intent.

4.3.2 Empirical Validation Protocol

The proxies are presented as theoretical constructs. A three‑stage validation protocol is specified for future empirical work (details in §4.3.4 of the full proposal):

· Stage 1 (Expert survey, n=20): Rendered designs spanning the AVI range [0.1, 1.0]; experts rate function transparency and expected user acceptance. Hypothesis: \rho(\text{AVI}, \text{perceived transparency}) > 0.6.
· Stage 2 (Naïve observer study, n=40): Video clips of each design in motion; participants predict the next foot placement. GILS is computed as prediction accuracy normalised by chance.
· Stage 3 (Amputee study, n=15): Users are fitted with covers spanning the AVI range; psychosocial adjustment is measured with the TAPES and OPUS instruments. Hypothesis: moderate AVI (0.4-0.7) maximises satisfaction (inverted‑U curve).

This protocol grounds the tectonic framework in measurable human outcomes, bridging the gap between architectural analogy and empirical design science.

4.4 Adaptability as a Design Philosophy

Following Brand (1994), we structure the leg’s adaptability into six layers: (1) kinematic structure (permanent), (2) mechanical structure (design lifetime), (3) outer shell (years, replaceable), (4) actuation system (months–years), (5) gait strategy library (days–weeks), and (6) real‑time terrain adaptation (milliseconds). This layered view ensures that design decisions at each timescale can be made independently.

We define the Gait Polyvalence Index to capture the leg’s versatility in a single number.

Definition 4.2 (Gait Polyvalence Index). Let \mathcal{T}(D) be the set of locomotion tasks (from the task‑scale pattern catalogue) for which a leg design D achieves a stable gait with specific resistance SR \le SR_{\text{threshold}}, and let m_D be the number of independent actuators in D. Then

GPI(D) = \frac{|\mathcal{T}(D)|}{1 + m_D}.

The denominator 1 + m_D avoids division‑by‑zero for purely passive designs (m_D=0), acknowledging that even a passive device requires one “design effort unit” in the form of careful engineering. A passive prosthetic foot that accomplishes walking and running yields GPI = 2/1 = 2; a single‑actuator ankle performing four tasks yields GPI = 4/2 = 2. Equal GPI indicates equivalent versatility per unit of actuation complexity. Distinctions among designs with similar GPI are then resolved on the multi‑objective Pareto frontier of (SI, SR, AVI), where legibility and stability play decisive roles.

