Chapter 6: Conclusion

6.1 Summary of Contributions

This dissertation has developed a unified theoretical framework that integrates geometric mechanics with locomotion‑design thinking for the analysis, representation, and generation of lower‑limb robot movements. The work is purely theoretical—no physical prototypes have been built, no simulations executed, and no user studies conducted. The contribution resides in the mathematical and design constructs themselves: the indices, theorems, grammars, pattern catalogues, legibility metrics, and formal mappings that together form a new interdisciplinary language for leg mobility. The seven principal contributions are as follows.

1. A Unified Geometric–Design Framework. We have coupled a hybrid port‑Hamiltonian description of the full gait cycle—continuous swing and stance flows linked by momentum reset maps at heel‑strike and toe‑off—with a suite of design‑oriented representations: a locomotion grammar, a hierarchical pattern language, and quantitative tectonic proxies. Unlike prior bipedal control frameworks (Ames et al. 2014; Duindam & Stramigioli 2006), this framework provides a vocabulary that is directly usable by prosthetists, rehabilitation engineers, and industrial designers while remaining faithful to the underlying Lie‑group geometry.

2. Formal Characterisation of the Stability–Adaptability Paradox. We introduced the Stability Index SI(P)=r/(n-r) and the Terrain Coupling Index TCI(P,g)=\|P P^+ g\|/\|g\|, and proved a two‑sided bounding theorem:

\frac{1}{\kappa(P)}\cdot\frac{r}{n-r}\cdot\frac{\|g_1\|}{\|g\|}
\;\le\; SI(P)\,TCI(P,g)
\;\le\; \kappa(P)\cdot\frac{r}{n-r}\cdot\frac{\|g_1\|}{\|g\|},

where \kappa(P) is the condition number of the coupling matrix. This theorem shows that the product of stability authority and terrain coupling is tightly constrained when the coupling is well‑conditioned, and that ill‑conditioning is what makes the paradox binding. The result provides mechanism designers with a precise, computable trade‑off surface that complements existing single‑joint stiffness analyses (Vanderborght et al. 2013).

3. A Geometric Locomotion Grammar. We defined a formal grammar G=(P,R,C) whose primitives are elements of SE(3), whose rules compose them temporally and bilaterally, and whose constraints enforce physical feasibility (joint limits, centre‑of‑pressure containment, friction‑cone conditions). The grammar bridges the gap between continuous attractor‑based CPG models (Ijspeert 2008) and abstract symbolic motion grammars, and a denseness argument shows that it can generate all standardised gaits to arbitrary precision.

4. A Hierarchical Gait Pattern Language. We organised locomotion knowledge into 22 patterns at three scales—joint, limb, and task—each documented with the Alexandrian template and linked to its mathematical realisation and contact‑wrench conditions. This catalogue captures the key regularities of human and robotic locomotion in a form that is directly usable for design specification, complementing the mathematical synergy analyses of Ivanenko et al. (2004).

5. Tectonic Proxies with Empirical Validation Protocol. We defined four quantitative legibility metrics—Actuator Visibility Index (AVI), Joint Articulation Exposure Score (JAES), Energy Flow Clarity (EFC), and Gait Intent Legibility Score (GILS)—and provided a complete three‑stage protocol (expert survey, naïve‑observer study, amputee trial) for their future empirical validation. This extends the concept of motion legibility (Dragan et al. 2013) to the morphological level and addresses a documented need in prosthetic design for functional transparency (Biddiss & Chau 2008; Jönköping University 2025).

6. Formal Mappings and a Synthesis Algorithm. We developed a rigorous translation methodology between mathematical descriptions (joint trajectories, coupling matrices, Hamiltonians) and design descriptions (pattern instances, grammar derivations, tectonic scores). Crucially, we provided a five‑step synthesis algorithm that converts a set of design‑pattern requirements into a target coupling matrix via SVD assignment, followed by mapping to a physical mechanism topology. This closes the loop from design intent to mechanism, making the framework actionable.

7. Theoretical Design‑Space Exploration. Using a 4‑DoF leg model, we derived analytical predictions directly from the energy balance and the index definitions. The specific resistance SR follows a U‑shape as a function of the ankle coupling ratio, with an optimal active‑passive energy split of approximately 28–35 %, independently matching the biological Achilles tendon contribution (Alexander 1988). The corrected design table shows three archetypal families—control‑intensive, passive‑dynamic, and hybrid—characterised by their rank r, SI, and SR. The orthogonality of the tectonic proxies to mechanical performance was demonstrated as a structural property of the framework.

Additionally, we repaired the Gait Polyvalence Index to GPI(D)=|\mathcal{T}(D)|/(1+m_D), removing the division‑by‑zero singularity for passive feet and providing a meaningful versatility‑per‑complexity metric. A computational appendix (Appendix F) provides working pseudocode for forward kinematics, index computation, energy estimation, grammar‑to‑trajectory instantiation, and pattern classification, ensuring that all theoretical constructs are algorithmically concrete.

6.2 Theoretical Implications

The synthesis of geometric mechanics and architectural design thinking that we have achieved is not a one‑off construction; it reveals a deep structural unity between the hand and the leg, between grasping and locomotion. The complete correspondence table (§5.4, Appendix E.1) demonstrates that every core concept in the hand dissertation—the coupling matrix, the indices, the grammar, the pattern language, the tectonic proxies, and the port‑Hamiltonian formulation—has a direct analogue in the leg framework. The inversion of the Adaptation Index into the Stability Index (SI = 1/AI) encodes the fundamental difference between manipulation (conforming to an object) and locomotion (resisting a surface) within the identical mathematical machinery. Impact events in locomotion are the analogue of grasp‑closure events in manipulation, both modelled by Dirac‑structure reset maps. The series‑elastic element in the leg plays the role of the tendon in the hand, storing and returning energy rather than amplifying force. And gait legibility, captured by GILS, generalises trajectory legibility to the morphological domain, showing that form and motion jointly communicate intent.

This unity suggests that the geometric‑design methodology is not specific to any single limb but constitutes a general approach to the analysis and synthesis of articulated biomechanical systems. The framework can, in principle, be extended to other locomotor appendages—wings, fins, serpentine bodies—wherever a coupling matrix can describe the mapping from actuators to degrees of freedom and a pattern language can capture the domain‑specific design knowledge.

6.3 Limitations and Future Research Directions

The entirely theoretical nature of this dissertation imposes specific limitations, each of which points to a concrete research programme. We enumerate them here in order of priority.

First and most immediate: computational validation of the analytical predictions. The U‑shaped SR curve, the optimal coupling ratio \rho^*, and the terrain‑recovery behaviour predicted in §3.5 must be confronted with a high‑fidelity simulation. The 7‑DoF planar biped model specified in the methodology (Chapter 3B) is designed for exactly this purpose: executing it in MuJoCo or a similar physics engine will convert the theoretical predictions into quantitative SR curves, stability margins, and terrain‑recovery data. This computational validation is the single step that moves the design‑space exploration from theoretical illustration to a validated computational framework, and it is the first priority for post‑doctoral work.

Second: Stage 1 of the tectonic validation protocol. The expert survey (n=20) described in §4.3.4 can be executed within a matter of months using high‑fidelity renderings of leg designs spanning the AVI range. It requires no physical hardware and will provide the first empirical evidence for or against the hypothesised correlation between actuator visibility and perceived functional transparency. Positive results would convert the AVI from a design hypothesis into a validated construct, opening the door to clinical studies.

Third: extension to full 3D gait. The current framework emphasises sagittal‑plane mechanics. A complete three‑dimensional treatment requires modelling the hip in SO(3) with all three rotational axes, the coupled frontal‑ and transverse‑plane dynamics, and the three‑dimensional subtalar axis. While algebraically straightforward in the POE formulation, this extension significantly expands the state space and the contact manifold, and it demands a corresponding enrichment of the pattern language to include lateral‑balance and pelvic‑rotation patterns.

Fourth: compliant and soft foot mechanics. The foot has been modelled as a multi‑segment rigid structure. Future work should incorporate continuous compliance—relevant to soft robotic feet and energy‑storing prosthetic keels—using either infinite‑dimensional port‑Hamiltonian formulations (Boyer et al. 2020) or pseudo‑rigid‑body approximations. The coupling matrix P would then become field‑valued, and the stability‑adaptability indices would require an infinite‑dimensional generalisation.

Fifth: variable transmission and clutch mechanisms. The bounding theorem demonstrates that the paradox is most binding when \kappa(P) is large and constant. Allowing the coupling matrix to vary across the gait cycle—for example, via a clutch that engages a high‑SI configuration during stance and releases to a low‑SI configuration during swing—is a natural resolution strategy that the hybrid port‑Hamiltonian structure directly accommodates. A systematic study of such mechanisms, including their optimal switching guards, would be a valuable extension.

Sixth: learning and adaptation. The locomotion grammar and pattern language could serve as structured priors for reinforcement‑learning‑based gait controllers. The discrete symbolic structure of the grammar constrains the policy search space in a human‑interpretable way, potentially enabling data‑efficient transfer across morphologies. Geometric deep learning on Lie groups (Finzi et al. 2023) is a natural technical partner for such an integration.

Seventh: whole‑body dynamics and bilateral coordination. The framework currently treats a single leg. Extending it to the coupled two‑leg system with pelvis and trunk dynamics is necessary for complete bipedal robot analysis. The Bilateral rule in the grammar provides the symbolic specification; the mathematical realisation requires a 14‑DoF or higher port‑Hamiltonian model with coordinated impact maps.

Eighth: formalisation of the Gait Polyvalence Index. The current operational definition GPI(D)=|\mathcal{T}(D)|/(1+m_D) is a practical metric, but a rigorous theoretical foundation—linking it to the volume of terrain‑parameter space for which stable gaits exist and to the indices SI and TCI—would significantly strengthen the framework’s quantitative core.

Ninth: higher‑order legibility and psychophysics. GILS should ultimately be linked to information‑theoretic measures of motion predictability and to psychophysical models of biological motion perception (Sciutti et al. 2018). Closing this gap would embed the tectonic proxies within a mature scientific literature on human motion understanding.

6.4 Concluding Remarks

This dissertation set out to answer the question: How can we develop a unified theoretical framework that integrates advanced mathematical modelling with architectural and design principles to analyse, represent, and generate lower‑limb robot movements? The answer we have provided is a framework in which every design concept—a heel‑strike absorption pattern, a terrain‑conformation strategy, an exposed series‑elastic spring—has a precise mathematical translation into Lie‑group kinematics, hybrid port‑Hamiltonian dynamics, and the singular value structure of a coupling matrix. Conversely, every mathematical object—a rank‑deficient P, a condition number, a Dirac reset map—carries a concrete design interpretation in terms of stability authority, terrain adaptability, and energy flow legibility.

The framework does not require the reader to choose between analytical rigour and design intuition. The two perspectives are formally mapped, and the synthesis algorithm of §5.2.2 demonstrates that one can begin with a design intent expressed in the pattern language and arrive at a target mechanism specification through a sequence of well‑defined algebraic steps. The indices SI and TCI provide a computable language for discussing the fundamental trade‑offs of legged mechanism design; the grammar and pattern language provide a human‑readable language for articulating design requirements and movement strategies.

We do not claim to have solved the problem of building an energy‑efficient, terrain‑adaptive, aesthetically coherent prosthetic leg. We claim, rather, to have provided the intellectual infrastructure within which such a leg can be systematically designed, analysed, and communicated across disciplines. The empirical and computational work that will test, refine, and extend this infrastructure lies in the future. The theoretical foundations laid here are offered as a platform for that work.

