A.1 Lie Groups and Screw Theory

The configuration space of a rigid body is the special Euclidean group

SE(3) = \{(R,\mathbf{p}) \mid R \in SO(3),\;\mathbf{p}\in\mathbb{R}^3\},

with group operation (R_1,\mathbf{p}_1)(R_2,\mathbf{p}_2) = (R_1R_2,\;R_1\mathbf{p}_2+\mathbf{p}_1). Its Lie algebra \mathfrak{se}(3) consists of twists

\mathcal{V} = \begin{bmatrix} \boldsymbol{\omega} \\ \mathbf{v} \end{bmatrix} \in \mathbb{R}^6,

identified with 4\times 4 matrices

[\mathcal{V}] = \begin{bmatrix} [\boldsymbol{\omega}] & \mathbf{v} \\ \mathbf{0}^\top & 0 \end{bmatrix},\qquad
[\boldsymbol{\omega}] = \begin{bmatrix} 0 & -\omega_3 & \omega_2 \\ \omega_3 & 0 & -\omega_1 \\ -\omega_2 & \omega_1 & 0 \end{bmatrix}.

The exponential map \exp([\mathcal{V}]\theta) yields an SE(3) transformation, with closed form

\exp([\mathcal{V}]\theta) =
\begin{cases}
\begin{bmatrix} I & \mathbf{v}\theta \\ \mathbf{0}^\top & 1 \end{bmatrix}, & \|\boldsymbol{\omega}\|=0,\\[6pt]
\begin{bmatrix} e^{[\boldsymbol{\omega}]\theta} & (I-e^{[\boldsymbol{\omega}]\theta})(\boldsymbol{\omega}\times\mathbf{v}) + \boldsymbol{\omega}\boldsymbol{\omega}^\top\mathbf{v}\theta \\ \mathbf{0}^\top & 1 \end{bmatrix}, & \|\boldsymbol{\omega}\|>0.
\end{cases}

A screw axis is a normalised twist:

\mathcal{S} = \begin{bmatrix} \boldsymbol{\omega} \\ \mathbf{v} \end{bmatrix},\quad
\|\boldsymbol{\omega}\|=1\;(\text{revolute}),\quad\text{or}\quad
\boldsymbol{\omega}=\mathbf{0},\;\|\mathbf{v}\|=1\;(\text{prismatic}).

For a revolute joint with axis \boldsymbol{\omega} passing through point \mathbf{q},

\mathcal{S} = \begin{bmatrix} -\boldsymbol{\omega}\times\mathbf{q} \\ \boldsymbol{\omega} \end{bmatrix}.

Product of Exponentials (POE). For a robot with n joints having screw axes \mathcal{S}_1,\dots,\mathcal{S}_n expressed in the spatial frame (at the home configuration) and a home end‑effector transformation M\in SE(3), the forward kinematics is

T(\theta) = \left( \prod_{i=1}^{n} e^{[\mathcal{S}_i]\theta_i} \right) M.

---

A.2 Port‑Hamiltonian Systems

A port‑Hamiltonian (pH) system on a state manifold \mathcal{X} with Hamiltonian H:\mathcal{X}\to\mathbb{R} is

\dot{x} = \big[ J(x) - R(x) \big] \nabla H(x) + g(x) u,\qquad
y = g(x)^\top \nabla H(x),

where J(x)=-J(x)^\top (interconnection), R(x)=R(x)^\top \ge 0 (dissipation), and g(x) is the input matrix. The power balance is

\dot{H} = - (\nabla H)^\top R (\nabla H) + y^\top u.

A mechanical system with configuration q\in\mathcal{Q} and momentum p has

H(q,p) = \frac12 p^\top M^{-1}(q) p + V(q),

with

J = \begin{bmatrix} 0 & I \\ -I & 0 \end{bmatrix},\quad R=0,\quad
g(q) = \begin{bmatrix} 0 \\ B(q) \end{bmatrix},

where B(q) maps actuator forces to generalised forces. Series‑elastic elements add spring coordinates \xi and a term \frac12 \xi^\top K_s \xi to H.

A hybrid pH system consists of a finite collection of modes, each a pH system, together with guards that trigger reset maps \Delta:\mathcal{X}^-\to\mathcal{X}^+.

---

A.3 Singular Value Decomposition and the Coupling Matrix

The actuation coupling matrix P\in\mathbb{R}^{n\times m} maps m actuator efforts to n joint torques (or actuator displacements to joint motions). Its singular value decomposition (SVD) is

P = U \Sigma V^\top,\qquad
U\in\mathbb{R}^{n\times n},\; V\in\mathbb{R}^{m\times m},\;
\Sigma = \begin{bmatrix} \Sigma_r & 0 \\ 0 & 0 \end{bmatrix},

with \Sigma_r = \operatorname{diag}(\sigma_1,\dots,\sigma_r), \sigma_1\ge\cdots\ge\sigma_r>0, and r = \operatorname{rank}(P). The Moore–Penrose pseudoinverse is

P^+ = V \Sigma^+ U^\top,\qquad
\Sigma^+ = \begin{bmatrix} \Sigma_r^{-1} & 0 \\ 0 & 0 \end{bmatrix}.

The condition number is \kappa(P) = \sigma_1 / \sigma_r \ge 1. The column space (actuated subspace) is \mathcal{C} = \operatorname{span}(U_r) where U_r contains the first r columns of U; the passive subspace is \mathcal{N} = \operatorname{span}(U_{n-r}). The orthogonal projection onto \mathcal{C} is P P^+ = U_r U_r^\top.

For any vector g\in\mathbb{R}^n, write g = g_1 + g_2 with g_1\in\mathcal{C} and g_2\in\mathcal{N}. Then g_1 = P P^+ g and \|g_1\|^2 = \sum_{i=1}^r (u_i^\top g)^2.

