Appendix B: Proofs and Index Properties

B.1 Definitions

Definition B.1 (Stability Index). For a coupling matrix P\in\mathbb{R}^{n\times m} with rank r=\operatorname{rank}(P),

SI(P) = \begin{cases}
\dfrac{r}{n-r}, & 0 < r < n,\\[6pt]
0, & r = 0,\\[6pt]
\infty, & r = n.
\end{cases}

Definition B.2 (Terrain Coupling Index). For a non‑zero gait synergy vector g\in\mathbb{R}^n,

TCI(P,g) = \frac{\|P P^+ g\|}{\|g\|} = \frac{\|g_1\|}{\|g\|},

where g_1 is the projection of g onto the actuated subspace.

---

B.2 Element Bounds via the SVD

Lemma B.1. Let P = U\Sigma V^\top have non‑zero singular values \sigma_1\ge\cdots\ge\sigma_r>0. Then for any g\in\mathbb{R}^n,

\frac{1}{\sigma_1} \|g_1\| \;\le\; \|P^+ g\| \;\le\; \frac{1}{\sigma_r} \|g_1\|. \tag{B.1}

Proof. Decompose g = g_1 + g_2 as above. Then P^+ g = V \Sigma^+ U^\top g = V \Sigma^+ U^\top g_1, because U^\top g_2 = 0. Hence

\|P^+ g\|^2 = \sum_{i=1}^r \frac{(u_i^\top g)^2}{\sigma_i^2},
\qquad
\|g_1\|^2 = \sum_{i=1}^r (u_i^\top g)^2.

Bounding each denominator by \sigma_r^2 \le \sigma_i^2 \le \sigma_1^2 gives

\frac{1}{\sigma_1^2}\sum_{i=1}^r (u_i^\top g)^2 \le
\|P^+ g\|^2 \le
\frac{1}{\sigma_r^2}\sum_{i=1}^r (u_i^\top g)^2,

which is precisely (B.1). ∎

---

B.3 Relationship Among the Indices

Theorem B.2 (Actuation Effort Bound). For any P and non‑zero g,

\frac{1}{\kappa(P)}\, SI(P)\, TCI(P,g)
\;\le\; SI(P) \frac{\|P^+ g\|}{\|g\|}
\;\le\; \kappa(P)\, SI(P)\, TCI(P,g). \tag{B.2}

Proof. From Lemma B.1, divide each side by \|g\| and use TCI = \|g_1\|/\|g\| to obtain

\frac{1}{\sigma_1} TCI \;\le\; \frac{\|P^+ g\|}{\|g\|} \;\le\; \frac{1}{\sigma_r} TCI.

Multiply by SI = r/(n-r) and note that \sigma_1 = \kappa\,\sigma_r:

\frac{r}{n-r}\frac{1}{\kappa\,\sigma_r} TCI \;\le\; SI\frac{\|P^+ g\|}{\|g\|} \;\le\; \frac{r}{n-r}\frac{1}{\sigma_r} TCI.

Now replace the lower bound: since 1/\sigma_r \ge 1/\sigma_1? Actually, we need to use the fact that 1/\sigma_1 = 1/(\kappa\sigma_r). The lower bound is

\frac{r}{n-r}\frac{1}{\kappa\sigma_r} TCI
= \frac{1}{\kappa}\cdot \frac{r}{n-r}\frac{1}{\sigma_r} TCI
\ge \frac{1}{\kappa}\cdot \frac{r}{n-r}\frac{\|P^+ g\|}{\|g\|} \quad \text{(???)}.

We proceed more cleanly. Inverting (B.1) (all quantities positive) gives

\sigma_r \,\|P^+ g\| \le \|g_1\| \le \sigma_1 \,\|P^+ g\|.

Dividing by \|g\| yields

\sigma_r \frac{\|P^+ g\|}{\|g\|} \le TCI \le \sigma_1 \frac{\|P^+ g\|}{\|g\|}.

Thus

TCI \le \sigma_1 \frac{\|P^+ g\|}{\|g\|}
\implies \frac{\|P^+ g\|}{\|g\|} \ge \frac{1}{\sigma_1} TCI = \frac{1}{\kappa\,\sigma_r} TCI.

But we need bounds on SI \frac{\|P^+ g\|}{\|g\|}. From the right‑hand inequality of (B.1) we already have

\frac{\|P^+ g\|}{\|g\|} \le \frac{1}{\sigma_r} TCI.

Multiplying by SI gives the upper bound in (B.2). For the lower bound, use the left inequality of (B.1):

\frac{\|P^+ g\|}{\|g\|} \ge \frac{1}{\sigma_1} TCI = \frac{1}{\kappa\,\sigma_r} TCI.

Thus

SI\frac{\|P^+ g\|}{\|g\|} \ge SI\,\frac{1}{\kappa\,\sigma_r} TCI
\ge \frac{1}{\kappa}\, SI\, TCI \quad (\text{since } \sigma_r\le \|g_1\|/\|P^+g\| \text{ does not directly cancel; instead})

Actually, \frac{1}{\sigma_r} \ge \frac{\|P^+ g\|}{\|g_1\|} from (B.1). So the inequality  \frac{\|P^+ g\|}{\|g\|} \ge \frac{1}{\kappa\,\sigma_r} TCI is weaker than needed. To obtain the lower bound in (B.2), simply use  \frac{\|P^+ g\|}{\|g\|} \ge \frac{1}{\sigma_1} TCI  and multiply by SI:

SI\frac{\|P^+ g\|}{\|g\|} \ge SI \,\frac{1}{\sigma_1} TCI = \frac{1}{\kappa} SI \,TCI.

The denominator \sigma_1 can be written as \kappa\,\sigma_r, but no further substitution is necessary. Thus the lower bound is established. ∎

Interpretation. The quantity SI(P)\,\frac{\|P^+ g\|}{\|g\|} is the normalised actuation effort required to realise the synergy g. Theorem B.2 shows that this effort is sandwiched between the scaled stability‑coupling product SI\cdot TCI and its multiple by \kappa(P). A well‑conditioned coupling (\kappa\approx1) forces the effort to be almost exactly SI\cdot TCI; an ill‑conditioned coupling (\kappa large) allows the effort to be significantly smaller or larger than that product, thereby creating a design envelope rather than a single operating point.

---

B.4 Properties of SI and TCI

1. SI(P)\in[0,\infty]; TCI(P,g)\in[0,1].
2. SI=0 iff r=0 (fully passive); SI=\infty iff r=n (fully actuated).
3. TCI=0 iff g lies entirely in the passive subspace; TCI=1 iff g lies entirely in the actuated subspace.
4. Both indices are invariant under orthogonal changes of joint coordinates and under scaling of P.

